  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 112 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nLater this week, a 500 mb trough will move into the pacific \nnorthwest, dislodging and merging with the west coast cutoff low \ncurrently blocking the overall pattern in the US. By Friday, this \nmerged 500 mb trough will push into the western CONUS and attempt to \nshove the ridge off eastward. However, this ridge becomes sandwiched \nbetween the western trough and another southeastern US trough, \nplacing Iowa right along the trough/ridge interaction. The net \nresult for Iowa will be a southwest flow regime aloft, with some \nincreasing moisture in both the low and mid-levels as a result. As \nenergy ejects out of the trough and along the ridge this weekend, \nIowa will see a few things result. First, tightened low-level \npressure gradients will promote breezy conditions both Friday and \nSaturday. The breeziest conditions will be over the northern and \nwestern parts of the state both days, and could result in some \nslightly elevated fire weather concerns over far northwest Iowa.\n\nPrecipitation chances also make a return this weekend, as a cold \nfront is pulled into the state by the surface low passing to our \nnorth. Iowa will be fairly well removed from the main synoptic \nforcing of the low, but ascent along the cold front combined with \nincreased moisture through the column will provide some potential \nfor rain Saturday into Sunday. Rain probabilities will be highest \nacross northwestern Iowa nearer to the main moisture transport axis \n(~40% chance), with lower likelihood where drier conditions are \npresent farther into central Iowa (10 to 20% chance). Southern and \neastern Iowa generally remain dry until the boundary slowly pushes \nfarther south and east into Sunday and Monday. Behind this boundary, \ntemperatures become more seasonal in the low 50s to upper 60s, \nalthough exact temperatures will be dependent on how prominent of a \ncold push results.",
